; dali_controller.loom
;
; Decisions (2026-11-03): IEC 62386-101 was not available; the figures
; come from Renesas R01AN4653 and Microchip TB3200, which cite edition
; 2.0. Two figures found in no public source (the 12.4 ms answer window
; and the 13.5 to 14.7 ms forward-frame spacing) are marked UNCHECKED in
; tools/protomodels/dali.py and must be checked against the standard.
;
; A DALI application controller (IEC 62386-101 and -102, the building
; lighting bus): it sends the forward frames the host asks for, listens for
; the control gear's backward frame when one is expected, and reports it.
;
; Written on 2026-11-02, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): DALI is in no design
; document. Hardware reused: the M2 core (pins, deadlines, timed waits,
; NOW, the FIFOs) and the bit engine's Manchester encoder (slice A,
; SEMANTICS 6.9.1, ENC = 2), built for the Manchester loopback of L3-MANCH
; at 2 Mbit/s and here run at 1200 bit/s, eight ticks a half-bit. No
; hardware knows DALI: the start bit, the 17-bit frame split over two loads
; of SR, the stop condition, the settling times before and after a frame,
; the listening window for the backward frame and its decoding are
; firmware. The decoding does not use SHI: SHI samples on the thread's
; fixed tick grid, and a DALI receiver must take half-bits from 333.3 to
; 500 us (-20 % to +20 %) inside one frame. It times every edge with NOW
; instead and classifies each interval, as the standard's receiver tables
; do (half-bit, double half-bit, violation, stop condition).
;
; Pins: TX = OUT0 to a DALI interface's transmit input, RX = IN0 from its
; receive output. Polarity: TX = 1 pulls the bus low (it switches the
; interface's shorting transistor on), TX = 0 releases it; RX is the bus
; level, 1 = high (idle, about 16 V on the wire), 0 = low. TX is active
; high so that a chip in reset or halted (PIN_OUT resets to 0) leaves the
; bus free: a bus held low for long is a system failure to every gear. The
; engine's INV bit makes the inversion; an interface with other polarities
; changes CFG, the two SETP TX lines and the JP and WAITE levels on RX.
; Every bit the controller sends comes back on RX (the bus is a wired AND).
;
; Line code (62386-101): bi-phase, a 1 is low then high and a 0 high then
; low, most significant bit first; that is IEEE 802.3's convention, which
; the engine's ENC = 2 sends as it is. Forward frame: start bit (1), address
; byte, data byte, then the stop condition (the bus high). Backward frame:
; start bit and 8 bits.
;
; Host command (INQ[0]), two words per frame:
;   word 1   bit 0 R: a backward frame is expected (a query); other bits
;            are ignored
;   word 2   the forward frame: bits 15:8 the address byte, 7:0 the data
;            or command byte
; Result (OUTQ[0]), two words per frame, always:
;   status   bits 7:0 the backward frame's byte (with bit 8); bit 8 BACK, a
;            valid backward frame; bit 9 FRAMING, something started in the
;            window but was no valid backward frame (a violation, or not 8
;            bits: two gears answering at once look like this, and 102 reads
;            it as "yes"); bit 10 NONE, nothing started in the window. All
;            zero when R was 0.
;   settle   the ticks from the end of the forward frame's last bit to the
;            backward frame's first edge (0 when none), Te/8 each.
;
; Timing, every figure from 62386-101 ed.2.0 (see firmware/README.md for
; the sources and which figures were not checked against the standard):
;   transmit  half-bit 8 ticks, exactly 416.7 us at the rate below (the
;             transmitter's limits are 366.7 to 466.7 us); every edge is
;             one slot after its tick (the second load of SR is made
;             between the two halves of the 16th bit, off the edges).
;   listen    from the end of the forward frame's last bit for 238 ticks (12.4 ms,
;             the receiver's settling window for a backward frame) for its
;             start bit, then the frame is decoded edge by edge.
;   settle    before every forward frame the bus must have been high, with
;             no edge, for 260 ticks (13.54 ms) since the end of the
;             previous frame, its own or a gear's: the priority-1 settling
;             time between frames is 13.5 to 14.7 ms, and 2.4 ms is the
;             least after a backward frame. A gear answering late is waited
;             out this way, never talked over.
;
; Decoding, in ticks of Te/8 (Te = the nominal half-bit, 416.7 us): an
; interval of 5 to 10 measured ticks is a half-bit, 11 to 21 a double
; half-bit, anything else a violation; no edge for 22 ticks with the bus
; high ends the frame. With one tick of quantisation that takes every
; half-bit from 260 to 570 us and every double half-bit from 570 to
; 1140 us, so the receiver's whole accept range (333.3 to 500, 666.7 to
; 1000 us) is inside, and the grey zones are split; 1200 us and more is a
; violation, as table 19 requires.
;
; Rate: the host writes TICK_INT and TICK_FRAC before RUN (the M2
; convention): Te/8 = 52.08 us, at 50 MHz TICK_INT 2604, TICK_FRAC 43
; (2604.17 clocks; half-bit 416.67 us). Every time in the program is in
; ticks, so a faster tick runs the same frames faster (the RTL tests use
; TICK 25). The deadline pairs are proved for ticks of 16 clocks or more
; (.tick below); 16 is also what lets the decoding loop (at most 16 slots
; from an edge back to its WAITE) finish before the shortest half-bit the
; classification takes (5 ticks). A slower tick only adds margin.
;
; Memory: 108 words of thread 0's quarter (0x000..0x06B), no data memory.
; Runs beside dali_gear on thread 1.
;
; Weaker than a dedicated DALI controller: one priority (13.5 ms between
; frames, priority 1) and no collision detection or multi-master
; arbitration (the 62386-101 ed.2.0 multi-master rules): it assumes it is
; the only controller on the bus; no 24-bit frames (62386-103 input
; devices); "send twice" configuration commands are the host's two
; commands, 13.5 ms apart, well inside the 100 ms the standard allows.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   TX = OUT0, RX = IN0
        .tick   16                      ; the fastest tick supported (header)
        .equ    CFG = (1 << 7) | (2 << 3) | (1 << 1)    ; INV, ENC Manchester, MSB first
        .equ    HALF = 8                ; ticks per half-bit
        .equ    HMIN = 5                ; a half-bit: 5 .. 10 ticks
        .equ    DMIN = 11               ; a double half-bit: 11 .. 21 ticks
        .equ    TEND = 22               ; no edge for this long: the frame ends
        .equ    LISTEN = 238            ; 12.4 ms: the backward-frame window
        .equ    SETTLE = 260            ; 13.54 ms of quiet bus before a frame
        .equ    BACK = 0x100            ; status bits
        .equ    FRAMING = 0x200
        .equ    NONE = 0x400

; Registers: r0 bits received, r1 bits counted, r2 the last edge's NOW,
; r3 an interval or scratch, r4 the decoder's phase or scratch, r5 the
; forward frame, r6 the command flags, r7 the settling ticks.

start:  SETP    TX, 0                   ; idle: the bus high
        .csr    BE_PINS, pin(TX), r4    ; out = TX (the engine reads no pin)
        CSRR    r2, NOW                 ; the first frame settles from here

; ---------------------------------------- the settling time, then a command
; r2 is the end of the last frame on the bus. Wait until SETTLE ticks after
; it with no edge and the bus high; every edge on the way starts it again.
quiet:  MOV16   r3, SETTLE
        ADD     r3, r3, r2
        CSRW    TD, r3
        WAITE   RX, ANY, T
        BNT     qedge                   ; an edge: the bus is not free yet
        JP      RX, 1, cmd              ; quiet and high: free
qedge:  CSRR    r2, NOW                 ; (or held low: wait on from now)
        JMP     quiet

cmd:    POP     r6                      ; word 1: R in bit 0
        POP     r5                      ; word 2: the forward frame
        LDI     r7, 0                   ; the settling ticks, if a reply comes

; -------------------------------------------------------- the forward frame
; SR first holds the start bit and the frame's bits 15:1, CNT 16, then the
; frame's bit 0 alone. Each half-bit is one SHO a slot after its tick.
        MOV     r3, r5
        SHRI    r3, 1
        MOV16   r4, 0x8000              ; the start bit, a 1
        OR      r3, r3, r4
        .csr    BE_CFG, CFG, r4         ; also clears HALF and FIRST (6.9.1)
        LDSR    r3
        LDI     r4, 16
        CSRW    CNT, r4
        MOV     r4, r5                  ; the frame's bit 0, for the second load
        SHLI    r4, 15
        SETD    1
        WAITD   0                       ; the start bit starts on a tick
        SHO                             ; its first half
ftx:    WAITD   HALF
        SHO                             ; a second half
        WAITD   HALF
        SHO                             ; a first half: the bit leaves SR and
        BNZ     ftx                     ; Z = (CNT == 0) after the 16th
        LDSR    r4                      ; SR is empty: the last bit, which the
        LDI     r3, 1                   ; second half (FIRST) does not need
        CSRW    CNT, r3
        WAITD   HALF
        SHO
        WAITD   HALF
        SHO                             ; the last bit
        WAITD   HALF
        SHO
        WAITD   HALF
        SETP    TX, 0                   ; the stop condition: the bus high
        CSRR    r2, NOW                 ; the end of the last bit (a slot ago)

; ------------------------------------------------------ the backward frame
        ANDI    r6, 1                   ; R; the status is 0 without it
        BZ      report
        MOV16   r3, LISTEN
        ADD     r3, r3, r2
        CSRW    TD, r3                  ; the end of the window
        WAITE   RX, FALL, T             ; a start bit, or the window is over
        BT      none
        CSRR    r3, NOW                 ; (the decoder's own read latency)
        SUB     r7, r3, r2              ; the settling ticks
        MOV     r2, r3                  ; the decoder times from this edge
        CALL    rxframe
        CMPI    r1, 9                   ; the start bit and 8 bits, no violation
        BNZ     bad
        SHLI    r0, 8                   ; the byte alone
        SHRI    r0, 8
        MOV16   r6, BACK
        OR      r6, r6, r0
        JMP     report
bad:    MOV16   r6, FRAMING
        JMP     report
none:   MOV16   r6, NONE                ; (r2 stays the end of the forward frame)
report: PUSH    r6
        PUSH    r7
        JMP     quiet

; ------------------------------------------------------------ the decoder
; rxframe: decodes one frame from its start bit's falling edge, whose NOW
; is in r2, by the time between edges (header). Returns r0 the bits, the
; newest in bit 0 (the start bit included), r1 their count, plus 32 for a
; violation, and r2 the end of the last bit (its mid-bit edge plus a
; half-bit for a frame that ends on a 1). r4 is the phase: 1 at a bit
; boundary, 0 at the middle of a bit, where the level after the edge is
; the bit. A violation returns at once; the caller waits the line out.
rxframe: LDI    r0, 0
        LDI     r1, 0
        LDI     r4, 1                   ; the start bit's first edge: a boundary
redge:  SETD    TEND
        WAITE   RX, ANY, T
        BT      rquiet                  ; no edge for TEND ticks
        CSRR    r3, NOW
        SUB     r3, r3, r2              ; the interval in ticks
        ADD     r2, r2, r3              ; r2 = this edge
        CMPI    r3, HMIN                ; C = (r3 >= 5)
        BNC     rviol
        CMPI    r3, DMIN                ; C = (r3 >= 11)
        BC      rdbl
        XORI    r4, 1                   ; a half-bit: the phase flips
        BZ      rbit                    ; now mid-bit: a bit
        JMP     redge                   ; now at a boundary: none
rdbl:   CMPI    r3, TEND
        BC      rviol                   ; 22 or more
        TEST    r4, r4                  ; a double half-bit from mid-bit to
        BNZ     rviol                   ; mid-bit only; from a boundary: violation
rbit:   SHLI    r0, 1
        JP      RX, 0, rb0              ; the level after a mid-bit edge
        INC     r0
rb0:    INC     r1
        JMP     redge
rquiet: JP      RX, 0, rviol            ; held low: no stop condition
        TEST    r4, r4
        BNZ     rend                    ; at a boundary: the last bit ended here
        ADDI    r2, HALF                ; mid-bit: it ends a half-bit later
rend:   RET
rviol:  ORI     r1, 32                  ; no frame length matches
        RET
